        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The Toronto Maple Leafs are no longer just reshuffling the bottom six; they've fundamentally altered their identity in the crease by <strong>locking up Sergei Bobrovsky to a three-year deal</strong>, a move that answers the question of why the veteran netminder chose Toronto over every other contender. This comes after a season that ended with the club sitting 8th in the Atlantic with just 78 points, a reality that forced the front office to pivot from hope to hard-nosed roster construction. Bobrovsky, now wearing No. 72, brings a winning pedigree that the Leafs desperately lacked, while the signing of Gavin McKenna to an entry-level contract signals a long-term commitment to youth development. The bottom six is still being fine-tuned, and Morgan Rielly's potential trade remains the final piece to watch. <strong>What to monitor next is whether Rielly departs before training camp and how the new crease chemistry evolves in August</strong>.",
